// The `#[foreign]` mark. It lives here rather than in the compiler's own tier
// because its users are outside the compiler: a language plugin generating
// marked SNGL to describe a foreign API, a platform package naming its host
// types.
import ir "sngl:internal/ir"

// Records what a declaration corresponds to outside SNGL: the import path it
// comes from and its name there, written the way an import line writes them.
// One argument is the name alone, which is all a struct field can say, since a
// field has no package of its own.
//
// The mark imports nothing, resolves nothing and validates nothing — a
// plugin's output is trusted — and it never confers type identity: several
// SNGL types can map to one target-language type, so two declarations naming
// the same foreign thing stay two types. Only a scheme importer's own origin
// unifies declarations.
//
// A marked declaration is still the program's own: a backend emits it, so the
// name is a name to spell alongside that declaration and never a reference
// redirecting to one the backend did not emit. Struct, struct field and
// function are the forms that carry it; the others refuse it. The flags that
// may follow the name are `ir.ForeignFlag`.
func foreign(path string, name string = "", flags list<ir.ForeignFlag> = []) ir.Macro

// Names the C identifier a declaration *is*: a function to call, or a type to
// spell. The declaration itself is emitted by nobody.
//
// Language-neutral on purpose. C is the one ABI several targets can reach, and
// a platform built on a C library has no business naming a SNGL language --
// `sngl:platform/gtk4` describes cairo with this and nothing Go-specific, so
// any backend that can call C can serve it. Today that is the Go one, which
// renders a call as cgo and supplies the `C.double`/`C.int` conversions from
// the declared parameter types.
//
// The name is spelled as the emitting language reaches it, prefix included
// where one is needed -- `C.cairo_line_cap_t` for a type, `cairo_arc` for a
// function the renderer prefixes itself.
//
// Struct and function are the forms that carry it, as `#[foreign]`'s are.
func cnative(name string) ir.Macro
